This package contains no LEI data. It contains a mapping: an ontology, a
connection descriptor, and three RML TriplesMaps that describe how to read two
Parquet files. Every LEI value a consumer sees is fetched from those files at
query time and is never copied into this repository or into the package archive.

The Parquet files themselves are a copy of GLEIF data, and Semvia publishes them
beside the archive in the public semvia-pkg bucket:

  https://pkg.semvia.io/semvia/gleif/@data/lei_records.parquet
  https://pkg.semvia.io/semvia/gleif/@data/relationship_records.parquet

They hold thirteen columns of the golden copy, unmodified apart from the column
selection and the row order. tools/export-parquet.py is the exact recipe.

The data those files hold is the GLEIF Golden Copy — Level 1 (who is who) and
Level 2 (who owns whom) — published by the Global Legal Entity Identifier
Foundation (GLEIF).

  Source:         GLEIF Golden Copy and Delta Files
                  https://www.gleif.org/en/lei-data/gleif-golden-copy/download-the-golden-copy
  Terms of use:   https://www.gleif.org/en/meta/lei-data-terms-of-use/

GLEIF's LEI Data Terms of Use state that the data available through their Access
Service is provided under the CC0 1.0 Universal licence. That is GLEIF's grant
over their data, not ours, and it is reproduced here as attribution rather than
as a representation — a consumer relying on it should read GLEIF's terms
directly, since GLEIF may change them.

"LEI", "GLEIF" and "Global LEI System" refer to GLEIF and its programme. This
package is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or produced in cooperation with
GLEIF.

The IRIs under https://www.gleif.org/ontology/ that this package's ontology
aligns to are GLEIF's published vocabulary terms. This package names them; it
does not redistribute their definitions, and installing it pulls in no GLEIF
file.

